===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To run [[quick]]ly or for a [[short]] distance.
#: {{syn|en|bolt|rush|sprint}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''dashed''' across the field.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|informal}} To [[leave]] or [[depart]].
#: {{syn|en|go|take off|Thesaurus:leave}}
#: {{ux|en|I have to '''dash''' now. See you soon.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[destroy]] by [[striking]] (against).
#: {{syn|en|beat|whack|Thesaurus:hit}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''dashed''' the bottle against the bar and turned about to fight.}}
#* {{RQ:Thoreau Cape Cod|page=14|passage=There were the tawny rocks, like lions couchant, defying the ocean, whose waves incessantly '''dashed''' against and scoured them with vast quantities of gravel.}}
#* {{RQ:Stoker Dracula|chapter=XXI|passage=Silence! If you make a sound I shall take him and '''dash''' his brains out before your very eyes.}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Tarzan of the Apes|chapter=IV|passage=Kala was the youngest mate of a male called Tublat, meaning broken nose, and the child she had seen '''dashed''' to death was her first; for she was but nine or ten years old.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To throw [[violent]]ly.
#: {{syn|en|chuck|feck|fling|sling|Thesaurus:throw}}
#: {{ux|en|The man was '''dashed''' from the vehicle during the accident.}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|para=792|passage=If you '''dash''' a stone against a stone in the bottom of the water, it maketh a sound.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XV|24|The rooks are blown about the skies;<br>The forest crack’d, the waters curl’d,<br>⁠The cattle huddled on the lea;<br>⁠And wildly '''dash’d''' on tower and tree<br>The sunbeam strikes along the world: […]}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|sometimes|figurative}} To [[sprinkle]]; to [[splatter]].
#: {{syn|en|dust|powder|sparge|scatter|speckle|strew}}
#* {{RQ:Thomson Spring|year=1768|passage=On each hand the gushing waters play, / And down the rough cascade white-'''dashing''' fall.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson In Memoriam|XXIV|41|The very source and fount of Day<br>Is '''dash’d''' with wandering isles of night.}}
#* {{RQ:Spectator|issue=262|date=31 December 1711|page=305|passage=[W]hen I draw any faulty character, I consider all those persons to whom the malice of the world may possibly apply it, and take care to '''dash''' it with such particular circumstances as may prevent all such ill-natured applications.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|dated}} To mix, reduce, or [[adulterate]], by throwing in something of an inferior quality.
#: {{syn|en|adulter|corrupt|debase|pollute|sophisticate|Thesaurus:adulterate}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''dash''' wine with water}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|of hopes or dreams}} To [[ruin]]; to [[destroy]].
#: {{syn|en|snuff|wreck|Thesaurus:kill|Thesaurus:destroy}}
#: {{ux|en|Her hopes were '''dashed''' when she saw the damage.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[dishearten]]; to [[sadden]].
#: {{syn|en|deject|depress|Thesaurus:sadden}}
#: {{ux|en|Her thoughts were '''dashed''' to melancholy.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|usually with {{m|en|down}} or {{m|en|off}}}} To complete hastily.
#: {{ux|en|He '''dashed''' down his eggs.}}
#: {{ux|en|She '''dashed''' off her homework.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[draw]] or [[write]] quickly; [[jot]].
#* {{RQ:Woolf Jacob's Room|chapter=I|passage="Scarborough," Mrs. Flanders wrote on the envelope, and '''dashed''' a bold line beneath; it was her native town; the hub of the universe.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|dated|euphemism}} [[damn|Damn]] {{q|in forming oaths}}.
#: {{syn|en|darn|eff|frig}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Dash''' his impudence! Who is that scoundrel?}}

